WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.199
This lesson covers stratified sampling for AI output review.

2
00:00:04.199 --> 00:00:07.400
We'll look at why random sampling misses what matters,

3
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.520
how to stratify by confidence, by category, and by stakes,

4
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.720
and how combining all three dimensions maximizes

5
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.879
the real errors your reviewers catch per hour.

6
00:00:17.879 --> 00:00:19.120
Let's get into it.

7
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.159
Random selection sounds fair,

8
00:00:21.159 --> 00:00:23.680
and that is exactly what makes it dangerous.

9
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.959
A queue built at random fills up almost entirely with outputs.

10
00:00:27.959 --> 00:00:29.799
The model already got right.

11
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.479
Stratified sampling is the alternative,

12
00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:36.040
a review selection strategy that deliberately oversamples

13
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.419
the outputs most likely to be wrong.

14
00:00:38.419 --> 00:00:41.479
Weighting selection toward low confidence outputs,

15
00:00:41.479 --> 00:00:44.759
rare categories, and high stakes decisions.

16
00:00:44.759 --> 00:00:48.080
Stakes weighting says the importance of an error

17
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.840
matters as much as the model's uncertainty.

18
00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.700
A confident wrong answer on a high stakes decision

19
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.840
is every bit as dangerous as an uncertain one

20
00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:58.819
on a routine task.

21
00:00:58.819 --> 00:01:00.700
Reviewer burnout is what happens

22
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.060
when neither of those is accounted for.

23
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.739
Flood people with correct output

24
00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:06.739
and they stop reading carefully,

25
00:01:06.739 --> 00:01:09.919
which is a system design failure, not a people problem.

26
00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:13.739
Here's the core problem with random sampling.

27
00:01:13.739 --> 00:01:16.419
If your model is 95% accurate,

28
00:01:16.419 --> 00:01:18.660
then a random sample of 100 outputs

29
00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.220
will contain roughly 95 correct ones and only five errors.

30
00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.220
Your reviewers spend most of their time

31
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.419
confirming what the model already knows,

32
00:01:27.419 --> 00:01:30.919
and the actual failure cases show up infrequently enough

33
00:01:30.919 --> 00:01:32.860
that patterns are hard to spot.

34
00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.199
Now scale that up.

35
00:01:34.199 --> 00:01:37.379
In a system processing 10,000 outputs per day,

36
00:01:37.379 --> 00:01:41.500
random review at a 1% rate gives you 100 reviews,

37
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.900
of which maybe five contain real errors.

38
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:47.459
You're burning reviewer time on low value confirmations.

39
00:01:47.459 --> 00:01:50.379
The errors you do catch are statistically isolated,

40
00:01:50.379 --> 00:01:53.379
so you can't identify the underlying cause.

41
00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:55.980
Random sampling gives you coverage on paper

42
00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.519
while hiding the actual failure distribution.

43
00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:02.019
Let's compare the two approaches directly.

44
00:02:02.019 --> 00:02:04.540
Random sampling is simple to implement.

45
00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:07.779
Every output has the same probability of selection,

46
00:02:07.779 --> 00:02:10.059
and no additional infrastructure is needed

47
00:02:10.059 --> 00:02:13.139
beyond a counter or a random number check.

48
00:02:13.139 --> 00:02:16.259
The downside is that the signal your reviewers receive

49
00:02:16.259 --> 00:02:19.220
is diluted by the model's existing accuracy.

50
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.020
Stratified sampling requires more setup.

51
00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.699
You need confidence scores, category labels,

52
00:02:24.699 --> 00:02:29.020
and a sampling policy that specifies rates for each stratum.

53
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.580
That's real engineering work, but the payoff is significant.

54
00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.699
Reviewers see a concentrated stream of uncertain,

55
00:02:35.699 --> 00:02:38.020
rare, or high-stakes outputs.

56
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.460
The error density in the queue goes up.

57
00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:43.339
Reviewers catch more real problems per hour

58
00:02:43.339 --> 00:02:46.220
and can identify patterns across cases.

59
00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.500
The upfront investment in stratification

60
00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.720
pays off quickly at any reasonable review volume.

61
00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:55.000
The most common starting point for stratification

62
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.679
is the confidence score.

63
00:02:56.679 --> 00:03:00.279
For a classifier, that score is the probability distribution

64
00:03:00.279 --> 00:03:01.919
it already outputs.

65
00:03:01.919 --> 00:03:05.199
For generative extraction, it's the verbalized self-report

66
00:03:05.199 --> 00:03:09.360
you elicit by asking, the same mechanism covered earlier,

67
00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.759
calibrated against a held-out set,

68
00:03:11.759 --> 00:03:13.800
so the numbers mean something.

69
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:17.440
Whatever the source, you set a threshold, say 0.7,

70
00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.839
and any output below that threshold

71
00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:21.960
gets sampled at a higher rate.

72
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.119
Outputs above the threshold still enter the review pool,

73
00:03:25.119 --> 00:03:27.000
but at a much lower rate.

74
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.320
The threshold itself is tunable.

75
00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:31.520
If reviewers have excess capacity,

76
00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.559
lower it to pull in more borderline cases.

77
00:03:34.559 --> 00:03:37.279
If they're overwhelmed, raise it to focus only

78
00:03:37.279 --> 00:03:39.440
on the lowest confidence outputs.

79
00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:42.279
Confidence stratification alone will dramatically

80
00:03:42.279 --> 00:03:44.759
improve your reviewers' error yield compared

81
00:03:44.759 --> 00:03:47.600
to a purely random baseline.

82
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.960
Stratification actually has three distinct dimensions,

83
00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:53.479
and effective systems use all three.

84
00:03:53.479 --> 00:03:54.919
The first is confidence.

85
00:03:54.919 --> 00:03:58.919
Anything below your threshold goes into a high-priority review lane.

86
00:03:58.919 --> 00:04:01.279
The second is category balance.

87
00:04:01.279 --> 00:04:04.919
If 80% of your outputs fall into one common category

88
00:04:04.919 --> 00:04:08.520
and only 5% land in a rare but important one,

89
00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:11.240
random sampling mirrors that imbalance,

90
00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:14.000
and the rare class stays underreviewed even when

91
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.239
its error rate is far higher.

92
00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:18.799
Per-category quotas fix this.

93
00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:20.559
You set a minimum number of reviews

94
00:04:20.559 --> 00:04:24.079
per category per day, independent of volume,

95
00:04:24.079 --> 00:04:27.040
which also surfaces drift early when a previously clean

96
00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.000
category starts failing.

97
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.079
The third dimension is stakes.

98
00:04:31.079 --> 00:04:34.079
Some outputs need review regardless of confidence

99
00:04:34.079 --> 00:04:36.600
because an error there is severe.

100
00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:40.119
These dimensions compose, so an output hitting two of them

101
00:04:40.119 --> 00:04:42.880
belongs at the top of the queue.

102
00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.399
Reviewer burnout deserves its own focus

103
00:04:45.399 --> 00:04:47.679
because it's a system design failure that

104
00:04:47.679 --> 00:04:49.799
looks like a personnel problem.

105
00:04:49.799 --> 00:04:53.040
When reviewers process hundreds of outputs per shift

106
00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.040
and the vast majority are correct,

107
00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:57.959
they develop a pattern of approving quickly.

108
00:04:57.959 --> 00:05:01.000
That's rational behavior given the signal they're receiving,

109
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.839
but it means that when a real error does appear,

110
00:05:03.839 --> 00:05:06.200
the reviewer is less likely to catch it.

111
00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.559
Their cognitive threshold has been recalibrated

112
00:05:08.559 --> 00:05:11.000
by the volume of correct outputs.

113
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.040
Stratification directly counters this

114
00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:16.119
by raising the error density of the review queue.

115
00:05:16.119 --> 00:05:18.440
When reviewers know that a meaningful fraction

116
00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.600
of every batch contains real issues, they stay engaged.

117
00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.760
The goal isn't to make the job harder.

118
00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.480
It's to make every review session

119
00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:28.399
worth the reviewer's full attention,

120
00:05:28.399 --> 00:05:32.239
rather than a rote confirmation exercise.

121
00:05:32.239 --> 00:05:36.000
Stratified sampling turns review from a coverage exercise

122
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.839
into a targeted quality signal, concentrating reviewer

123
00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:44.200
attention on uncertain, rare, and high-stakes outputs,

124
00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.040
raises the number of real errors caught per hour,

125
00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.440
and gives you a much clearer picture of where

126
00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.559
the model actually struggles.

127
00:05:51.559 --> 00:05:53.839
The upfront work of scoring, labeling,

128
00:05:53.839 --> 00:05:56.799
and setting sample rates pays for itself quickly

129
00:05:56.799 --> 00:05:59.519
at any serious review volume.

130
00:05:59.519 --> 00:06:01.679
With that, we'll wrap up this lesson.

131
00:06:01.679 --> 00:06:04.079
I'll see you in the next one.

